# DBW LEAD RESEARCH SKILL

## PURPOSE
Pull qualified construction contractor companies
from available sources for a given rep territory
and city cluster. Apply all targeting and
disqualification filters. Return only companies
that meet full qualification standards.

## QUALIFICATION STANDARDS
Every company must meet all of the following
before being passed forward as a lead candidate.

The company must be a self-performing contractor
with crews actively on site using saws and blades.
The company must fall within the size range of
2 to 500 employees and $1M to $20M in revenue.
The company must be headquartered or actively
operating in the rep's assigned territory and
city cluster for today's run.
The company must not have been delivered as a
lead in any previous run for this rep.
The company must not be on the auto-skip list.

## TARGET COMPANY TYPES
Primary targets — always fill from here first:
Concrete contractors, roadway and DOT contractors,
curb and gutter crews, bridge contractors, airport
and runway contractors, commercial concrete crews
that are self-performing, asphalt and paving
contractors, masonry contractors, site work
contractors, utility and underground contractors,
and demolition contractors.

Secondary targets — use to fill remaining slots
if Primary cannot reach 5 leads:
Core drilling companies.

Low priority — use only as a last resort to fill
a gap when Primary and Secondary together fall
short of 5 leads:
Professional concrete cutting and sawing specialty
firms. Note that these contacts are technically
sophisticated, negotiate extremely hard, frequently
return product, and routinely give reps difficulty
after the sale. They are harder closes with higher
rep risk. Never pull as a first choice.

Auto-skip these company types under all circumstances:
General contractors who subcontract all field work,
architects, engineers, supply houses, equipment
rental companies, developers, and consultants.
The simple test is: if they do not have guys on
site running saws, skip them without exception.

## SOURCE ORDER
Pull from sources in this exact sequence. Move to
the next source only when the current one returns
fewer than 5 qualified candidates after filtering.

ZoomInfo first: Search Companies using NAICS codes
238140, 237310, and 238110 filtered to the rep's
assigned city cluster and size range of 2 to 500
employees. Pull 8 to 10 candidates to allow for
disqualification after filtering.

ConstructConnect second: Pull active or recently
awarded projects in the territory. Extract every
bidding subcontractor listed. Each bidder is a
potential candidate to run through the qualification
filter above.

Tango third: Search DOT and government awarded
contracts in the territory. Pull all active
contractor names from the results and run them
through the qualification filter.

Free web sources last resort only: Use only if
the three sources above combined cannot produce
5 qualified leads. Search using any combination
of Google, LinkedIn company search, Facebook and
Instagram business pages showing active jobsite
work, Manta.com, BBB.org, and Bluebook.com.
Any result that surfaces a real company name,
address, and verifiable phone number is a valid
candidate to run through the qualification filter.

## OUTPUT
Return a list of exactly 5 qualified company names
with their full address, phone number, website,
company type, revenue estimate, employee count,
and the source that found them. These companies
are then passed to dbw-contact-enrichment.skill
for contact enrichment in the next step.